# ==============================================================================
# env.defaults — the PUBLIC half of the shipped server configuration.
# Every value in here is safe to publish: identifiers, URLs, ports, feature flags,
# tuning knobs, and the two tokens that are public BY DESIGN (see the annotations
# on INDEXNOW_KEY and AGENT_FREE_TOKEN).
#
# What grants access lives in deploy/env.secrets — same format, NOT in git. A new
# server gets both halves merged into .env by scripts/make-env.sh (which setup-all.sh
# and install-from-usb.sh call), so `npm install` still needs no manual editing.
#
# Why the split exists: this file used to carry the live tunnel token, the Cloudflare
# API token, the admin credential and the backup upload secret. That made the
# repository permanently un-publishable — and a tunnel token is not a mild leak: anyone
# holding it can point tukuk.org at their own machine, because the edge trusts the
# token, not the server.
# ==============================================================================

# ── Admin (email + passkey) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ADMIN_EMAIL + ADMIN_PASSWORD are in env.secrets — together they are the bootstrap
# path to the passkey registration, so neither belongs in a publishable file.
ADMIN_SESSION_TTL_MS=28800000

# ── WebAuthn ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Quoted on purpose: the value contains a space. Unquoted, a script that does
# `set -a; . ./.env` runs the trailing word as a command (`Admin: command not found`) — which is
# how `WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=Tukuk-OS Admin` broke every ops script that sourced this file on 31 Oct
# 2026 while systemd and src/config.js were perfectly happy. src/config.js strips the quotes
# (loadEnvFile), so the app sees the same string either way.
WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME="Tukuk-OS Admin"
WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=tukuk.org
WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=https://tukuk.org,https://www.tukuk.org

# ── IndexNow (traffic: Bing/Yandex/Naver/Seznam get URLs immediately) ─────
# The key is hosted publicly at https://tukuk.org/<KEY>.txt (file public/bd8f0317…txt).
# 1 = every newly published article from /admin is sent straight to IndexNow.
# Public by design — IndexNow fetches it from the site, so hiding it is not an option.
INDEXNOW_ENABLED=1
INDEXNOW_KEY=bd8f0317c5ab4cddb9a3dc62f7ec15ae

# ── Cloudflare Tunnel (TOKEN mode — recommended, no credentials file) ──────
# The token itself is in env.secrets (CF_TUNNEL_TOKEN). The ID below is only an
# identifier: it names which tunnel the routes belong to and grants nothing on its
# own, so it stays here where the docs and the audit script can read it.
# How to mint a new token: Cloudflare → Zero Trust → Networks → Tunnels → the tunnel
# → copy its token (or `cloudflared tunnel token <UUID>` with cert.pem).
# setup-all.sh in TOKEN mode writes config.yml (token + ingress) from the
# deploy/cloudflared/config.yml template — the tukuk.org / www / dashboard INGRESS
# is already there, so there is NO need to create a "Public Hostname" in the dashboard.
# A new server therefore needs the code (clone or USB) + the secrets file + `npm
# install` — the tunnel comes up automatically, no .json file, no GitHub auth.
# (The older named mode needs a <uuid>.json file — tunnel 168a53af... is
#  dead because its credentials were lost when the old server was deleted.)
CF_TUNNEL_ID=c1d8ea0f-b512-487b-b7e7-ad730c24da9e

# ── Cloudflare API (for wrangler: www worker + R2 snapshot) ───────────────
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN (cfat_…) is in env.secrets. Token: My Profile → API Tokens →
# Create (template "Edit cloudflare Workers & Pages" + add R2 & Zone DNS permissions).
# With it present, every `npm install` auto-deploys the worker & enables the 6-hour
# snapshot timer; without it those two steps are skipped and say so.
# Measured 31 Oct 2026 with the token named `1-dasar-kebenaran-30-10-2026`: Zone READ,
# tunnel READ + token, Workers READ, R2 READ+EDIT ✓. It CANNOT edit DNS, CANNOT edit
# Workers Routes (so it cannot remove the `tukuk.org/*` → tukuk-fallback route the
# account moved in with — README §9), and cannot purge cache. The old `cfut_…` token is
# revoked with the old account — do not chase it.
# A SECOND credential now exists: `CLOUDFLARE_DNS_API_TOKEN` (in env.secrets) = Zone
# tukuk.org · DNS · Edit. It can list/create/update DNS records (used to add the missing
# `tchat` / `play` / `build` CNAMEs on 31 Oct 2026) but, like the first token, it has no
# Workers Routes and no tunnel-route scope, so it cannot finish the edge by itself.
# Measured, not assumed: with that token `GET /zones/{Z}/workers/routes` answers
# `403 No access to the specified resource` — it is DNS-scoped only, so the stale apex
# route has to go through the dashboard (or a token granted Zone · Workers Routes · Edit).

# ── Tukuk Agent (/api/agent/* tool-calling proxy + build API) ───────────────
# AGENT_TOKEN = the Bearer used by the Cline extension inside Tukuk Agent to
# call https://tukuk.org/api/agent/v1. It is in env.secrets (an admin passkey session
# is also accepted). Do not put the real value back here.
# Editor origin (agent.tukuk.org) allowed to call tukuk.org via CORS.
AGENT_ORIGIN=https://agent.tukuk.org
# AGENT_FREE_TOKEN = the FREE-tier marker for Tukuk Builder (build.tukuk.org).
# This value is DELIBERATELY public: it is compiled into the browser bundle
# (tukuk-builder providers/tukuk.ts), so it cannot be a secret. Its protection is a
# strict rate limit (default 8/min/IP) + max_tokens constraints (default 16384).
# Real cost = our rotating cloud quota; this filter only prevents large-scale siphoning.
# It must NEVER equal AGENT_TOKEN. src/agent.js checks AGENT_TOKEN first, so one shared
# value hands every free Builder client the full tier: the rate limit and the max_tokens
# clamp never apply, and the cloud quota we pay for is what drains. Until 2026-10-31 the
# two WERE the same string, in three tracked files — both values are rotated, and the
# comparison test in test/secret-hygiene.test.js fails the build if a value from
# env.secrets ever reappears in a tracked file.
AGENT_FREE_TOKEN=da0860f427988f1b70706cc8c41a25dd7feec59cba975b6db755f32794539d6f
# AGENT_FREE_RATE_PER_MIN=8
# AGENT_FREE_MAX_TOKENS=16384
# LLM_CLOUD_ORDER = the provider rotation order for the builder AGENT (only
# kind:'openai' + tools-capable providers are used; gemini/cloudflare/litert are SKIPPED).
# GROQ IS BANNED (owner veto): 'groq' must NEVER appear in this list, and a stale
# GROQ_API_KEY in .env is ignored. The veto is enforced in code by LLM_DISABLED_PROVIDERS.
# VPS tukuk.org production value (2026-10-30 — free-only, groq removed):
#   LLM_CLOUD_ORDER=gemini-openai,openrouter,cloudflare,ollama-cloud
# Note: 'gemini-openai' = Google's OpenAI-compatible layer (function-calling OK, large free
# tier); ACTIVE ONLY when GEMINI_API_KEY is set (get a free key at aistudio.google.com).
# 'huggingface' (Inference Providers) returns 402 with zero credits — re-add the name to
# the order once credits/PRO are active. (The HF *model hub* is still used for storing
# our own trained models — that path is scripts/hf-model-pull.sh, unrelated to 402.)
# LLM_DISABLED_PROVIDERS=groq
#   Hard veto list (comma separated). Default already 'groq' — nothing to do. An empty
#   value lifts the veto; do not do that on tukuk.org.
# LLM_CLOUD_ORDER=gemini-openai,gemini,ollama-cloud,openrouter,xai,cloudflare,openai,litert

# ── Own models: Kaggle GPU training → Hugging Face hub → server ─────────────
# HF_TOKEN  = write token for huggingface.co (needs 'write' role) so a Kaggle kernel
#             can publish our trained model. The token travels as a KAGGLE SECRET,
#             not in this file. On the server it is only needed to DOWNLOAD
#             (public repos need nothing).
# KAGGLE_ID = Kaggle username (kaggle-gpu-burst.sh). Never put kaggle.json here.
# HF_TOKEN=
# KAGGLE_ID=

# ── Git branch used on the server ──────────────────────────────────────
GIT_BRANCH=main
# Daily auto-update (~04:30) on ALL servers: git pull → npm install →
# service + worker + snapshot refreshed in one pass. Set 0 to disable.
GIT_AUTO_UPDATE=1

# ── Local AI tier (Ollama + LiteRT-LM) — POLICY: OFF on the VPS ─────────────────
# The DEFAULT is already off (src/config.js: localFirst=false, litertHost=''), so there
# is no need to write anything here. Do NOT set BRAIN_LOCAL_FIRST=1 / LITERT_HOST
# unless the local tier is actually ENABLED (systemctl enable --now
# litert-lm / ollama after scripts/setup-litert-pilot.sh passes its benchmark).
# Reason on tukuk.org (6.8 GB, no GPU): ollama has no model (~/.ollama/models
# does not exist yet) and litert-lm was turned off after the pilot — its unit is disabled but
# .env once set both these values, which only fooled the admin
# panel ('local-first' was shown while every request went to the cloud).
# The local tier also sits at the VERY BACK of LLM_CLOUD_ORDER (slowest),
# so it has no latency benefit — only extra RAM risk.
# BRAIN_LOCAL_FIRST=1        # (enable ONLY together with the local service running)
# LITERT_HOST=http://127.0.0.1:8090/v1
# ── Tukuk Agent (editor /agent, port 8443) ──────────────────────────────────
# The tukuk-agent.service MUST be enabled+active: tukuk-os proxies
# /agent/* to it (src/agent-editor.js); if it is down, tukuk.org/agent → 502.
# The release is rsynced to /opt/tukuk-agent/release (536 MB, gitignored — an artifact).
# This unit reads EnvironmentFile=/home/tukuk/.config/tukuk-os/env for
# AGENT_TOKEN + the editor password — that file needs to be created once on the server.

# ── Mutation-route key (index & crawler) ────────────────────────────────────
# POST /crawl, /crawl/batch, /crawl/sitemap, /crawl/site, /documents,
# DELETE /documents/:id and POST /settings are guarded by requireApiKey(): if
# API_KEY is EMPTY, that middleware is fail-open and anyone on the internet could
# inject into / empty out our search index. setup-all.sh generates a random key
# and writes it to .env (the live value lives in the server's .env, NOT here), so
# a new install is safe by default. Local scripts send the X-API-Key header.
# API_KEY=< generated by scripts/setup-all.sh >

# ── Live traffic tiles + boot warm-up ───────────────────────────────────────
# Empty traffic vars = the keyless default (Google raster, flow overlay burned in).
# Keyed provider = set TRAFFIC_TILE_URL (must contain {apiKey}) + TRAFFIC_API_KEY
# + TRAFFIC_SOURCE_LABEL, then restart. A keyed template without a key is read as
# "no provider configured", never as a wall of 403s. See src/traffic-live.js.
TRAFFIC_TILE_URL=
TRAFFIC_API_KEY=
TRAFFIC_SOURCE_LABEL=
# WARMUP=0 turns off the post-restart page warm-up (src/warmup.js).
WARMUP=
WARMUP_PATHS=

# ── Off-site restore (scripts/rebuild-from-offsite.sh) ────────────────────────
# Two of the three values this step needs are here on purpose, and the reason is a
# circularity: the identity archive is only useful if a brand-new box can reach it
# without already having the old machine. BACKUP_WORKER_URL + CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID
# are endpoints/identifiers, so publishing them grants nothing.
# BACKUP_UPLOAD_SECRET is the one credential in that trio, so it is in env.secrets —
# which means a fresh box needs the secrets file before it can restore from off-site.
# That is the intended trade: the secrets file is the single out-of-band object the
# operator carries (USB, or pasted once), and the repository is no longer it.
# BLANKED 31 Oct 2026: `tukuk-backup` lived in the OLD account and was not carried over,
# so the URL answered Cloudflare 1042 forever. Left empty on purpose — every upload script
# then takes the R2-direct branch (`wrangler r2 object put`), which the current token is
# allowed to do and which spends no Worker requests. Re-set it only if an upload worker is
# actually deployed in CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID below.
BACKUP_WORKER_URL=
CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID=6134c115f7311412314b35f3ccf76166

# ── ONE tunnel: play.tukuk.org / tchat / build are routes on CF_TUNNEL_ID ────
# There is deliberately no CF_BUILD_TUNNEL_ID / CF_BUILD_TUNNEL_TOKEN any more. The
# second connector (a3af5bb4) is retired: its hostnames are routes on the one tunnel
# above, added in the Zero Trust dashboard. A key for a second tunnel is a standing
# invitation to recreate one.

# ── Google ads: the publisher account of record ───────────────────────────────
# The AdSense + AdMob account that monetizes tukuk.org (console: status Open, active
# products AdMob + Content, business domain tukuk.org VERIFIED). Held by
# tukuk.co@gmail.com; the legal entity Google has on file is "tukuk trading plt"; the
# Customer ID to quote in any Google support thread is 5990814777. None of that is
# secret: the publisher id travels in every ad request the page makes and is the first
# line of public/ads.txt, so writing it here protects nothing and lets a fresh box serve
# ads — and pass Google's third verification method (the `google-adsense-account` meta)
# — with no operator in the loop. The retired personal account's ids were removed
# 2026-11-01; this is the replacement, and it is also what ads.txt/app-ads.txt name.
ADSENSE_CLIENT=ca-pub-2797331455354322
# The approval gate is deliberately NOT set here, and that is not an oversight.
# src/config.js reads ADSENSE_APPROVED from .env only, and test/crawl-budget.test.js
# pins it to false when the key is absent: the gate exists so an account that Google has
# not approved can never ship ad markup, because ad units on thin pages are the violation
# that gets accounts banned. The account IS approved now, so the switch is a per-box
# operator action, one line, never a repo default:
#   echo 'ADSENSE_APPROVED=1' | sudo tee -a /opt/tukuk-os/.env && sudo systemctl restart tukuk-os
# Until that line exists, every gate in adsenseOn() stays shut and no page loads
# pagead2.googlesyndication.com. (Verified 2026-11-02: the live box HAS the line — /blog
# serves three <ins> units with this client, so ads are on in production already.)
# The three slot ids below are the ones the live Admin store holds and that every visitor's
# page already carries in its HTML, so they are public, not secret. They are recorded here so
# a rebuilt box can be compared against what is really running; Admin still wins over env.
#   top / bottom: every server-rendered content page  ·  in-article: blog posts only
# The homepage and every SPA page use auto ads and need only the client id above.
ADSENSE_SLOT_TOP=6911764298
ADSENSE_SLOT_IN_ARTICLE=8510244432
ADSENSE_SLOT_BOTTOM=7197162760

# ── The single Google tag + Clarity ──────────────────────────────────────────
# GA4 property "tukuk-org-web" (property id 561854360, web data stream id 16251234684,
# measurement id below), installed through its MEASUREMENT id — the property looked empty
# because nothing ever pointed at it: the code used to hardcode a GTM container
# (GT-PZXWTWW8) and the SPA loader only ever understood container ids. One tag per page, and
# the field takes either form, enforced in src/server.js googleTagHead() and mirrored in
# public/adsense-loader.js:
#   G-…   → Google's "install manually" gtag.js (what is configured now, no container)
#   GTM-/GT- → the container at tagmanager.google.com, with GA4 configured INSIDE it
# Both at once would double-count every pageview, so there is exactly one id and one
# loader. The name below is legacy (it predates the GA4 option) — the value is not a
# container. Ids of this kind are public by design (each is baked into the tag URL it
# loads), so a default here is safe; Admin's `gtmId` wins over it UNLESS the stored value is
# the retired container (resolveGoogleTagId treats that as "never configured" — set
# GTM_KEEP_LEGACY_ID=1 only if that container is genuinely yours), and the `gtmEnabled`
# toggle strips the tag from every page instantly. Strict-CSP pages
# (about/privacy/terms/docs) carry no tag at all, by design.
#
# Google Ads account 622-127-4131 (tag id AW-18551010096) is LINKED to this property, and
# conversions/remarketing ride the same tag: add it as a DESTINATION of G-HLPDZPEYMN in the
# Google tag console ("Manage Google tag → destinations"), never as a second <script> here —
# a pasted AW- snippet alongside this one is exactly the double-count the rule above forbids.
# The Cloudflare "Google tag gateway" for the zone is enabled and must be pointed at the same
# single id (measurementId below). If its "set up the tag automatically" option is on, the
# edge injects a second copy of the tag into every HTML response, so pages would carry two;
# with this repo installing the tag itself, that option belongs off and the gateway stays a
# proxy only. Neither the DNS nor the account token in vault can read or write Zone Settings,
# so this is a dashboard change (zone → Settings → Google Tag Gateway) or a token with
# Zone · Settings: Edit.
GTM_CONTAINER_ID=G-HLPDZPEYMN
# Microsoft Clarity (session replay, disclosed in /privacy) — public id, same posture,
# Admin-overridable via the `clarityEnabled`/`clarityId` settings.
CLARITY_PROJECT_ID=ytshvg0zer

# ── Firebase: the project of record (nothing in this repo reads these yet) ──────────────
# Recorded 2026-11-02 from the console, so a rebuilt box and the next developer know which
# Firebase project belongs to tukuk.org instead of guessing from a screenshot. The audit that
# justifies the heading: `grep -rli firebase src/ public/ deploy/ scripts/` returns only
# `src/pangkalan-tentera-data.json` (Vietnamese place names carried from a wiki dump —
# "Firebase Crook", "Firebase Gio Linh") and this file. No SDK, no config object, no import.
# What tukuk.org already has, and what is easy to mistake for FCM while reading the console's
# "Sijil Web Push" screen, is its own push channel: `src/chat.js` mints an EC P-256 VAPID pair
# on first use, stores it in the chat JSON state and PUTs an empty body to each subscriber's
# push-service endpoint (`/api/chat/vapid-public-key` hands out the public half; T Chat's
# client subscribes with it). That is plain Web Push, not Firebase Cloud Messaging — it needs
# no project, sender id or certificate. So the console's key pair is unused here, and pasting
# the Firebase snippet would buy a duplicate push stack plus the double-tag problem below.
# Project id is `tukuk-org-web` — that is what the SDK config itself states (authDomain,
# storageBucket and the service-account address all carry it); the console's project *name*
# and the linked Hosting site read `web-org-tukuk`, the same string with the words rotated.
FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=tukuk-org-web
FIREBASE_PROJECT_NUMBER=342129340504
FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=tukuk-org-web.firebaseapp.com
FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=tukuk-org-web.firebasestorage.app
FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=342129340504
# Web app "TUKUK OS".
FIREBASE_WEB_APP_ID=1:342129340504:web:b60855fc50c0a548fd9a2d
# THE ONE THAT MATTERS: Firebase's own measurementId is G-HLPDZPEYMN — the same id as
# GTM_CONTAINER_ID above, because the Firebase project and the GA4 property are the SAME
# measurement (GA4 property 561854360 lists Firebase project 342129340504 as linked).
# Consequence, and it is the reason the console snippet was NOT pasted into any page here:
# `getAnalytics(app)` loads gtag.js for that measurement id by itself. Dropping the
# console's <script type="module"> block into a page that already carries googleTagHead()
# installs TWO Google tags and counts every pageview twice — precisely what
# test/google-tag.test.js exists to fail the build over. Choose one: this repo's tag, or
# Firebase's, never both on the same page.
FIREBASE_MEASUREMENT_ID=G-HLPDZPEYMN
# Cloud Messaging: HTTP v1 ENABLED, legacy HTTP/XMPP disabled — the correct posture, since
# the legacy APIs were cut off 20/6/2024. Nothing sends through it yet, because chat push
# rides the self-hosted VAPID key described above. The web-push certificate pairs with the app
# id and is public by design (a VAPID-style public key, sent with every subscribe), so it is
# safe to know about — but two push stacks on one site is a reason to retire one, not to add
# the other.
# Admin SDK service account. Its ADDRESS grants nothing alone, so it can be written here;
# the KEY must not be. A generated service-account JSON is full read/write over Firestore,
# Storage, Auth and Messaging for this project — the same class of object as the tunnel
# token, and it is handled the same way: a 600 file on the box, named by a path variable,
# gitignored by filename (see .gitignore), with test/secret-hygiene.test.js asserting that
# ignore rule so a careless `git add -A` cannot publish it.
FIREBASE_ADMIN_CLIENT_EMAIL=firebase-adminsdk-fbsvc@tukuk-org-web.iam.gserviceaccount.com
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_FILE=
# The web API key (AIza…) is deliberately absent, and that is not shyness about a public
# value: a Firebase web key IS meant to ship in browser code, but `test/secret-hygiene.test.js`
# treats every AIza… string in a tracked file as live-shaped, and right now no page loads
# Firebase at all, so recording it would weaken the guard for zero benefit. Add it to
# env.secrets on the box the day a Firebase client is actually wired up.

# ── Cloudflare Workers: the "no Workers connected" screen is the GOAL ───────────────────
# The zone's console page offering to "create a Worker / connect an existing Worker" to
# tukuk.org must be left alone — it is empty because that is the design, not because something
# is missing. Measured 2026-11-02: `GET /zones/<ZONE>/workers/routes` through
# CLOUDFLARE_API_TOKEN (the `cfat_…` account token) answers `"result": []` — zero routes on the
# zone — and that same token lists exactly two scripts in the account, `email-routing-template`
# (Email Routing / MX, keep) and `workflows-starter-template`, neither on the web path. This
# closes the `tukuk.org → (script null)` ghost the ops runbook §3 recorded on 2026-11-01: the
# apex is tunnel-only again, confirmed by `GET /health` answering live Node JSON instead of the
# fallback worker's `"API unavailable — server offline"`.
# Mind which token is being quoted when re-checking: the DNS token (`cfut_…`,
# CLOUDFLARE_DNS_API_TOKEN) gets `"No access to the specified resource"` even reading routes —
# writing them needs *Zone → Workers Routes → Edit*, which neither token has.
# Keep it workerless for the reasons in README §9: a route on the apex makes the origin
# unreachable however healthy cloudflared is, and a Worker that fetches its own zone loops into a
# zone-wide 429 (error 1027) while spending the account's 100k/day free Worker requests on
# ordinary visitors. `worker/wrangler.toml` keeps `routes = []` for exactly this reason.
# Same heading covers the two "connect something" offers that must stay unfilled: no Firebase
# Hosting record (the console's linked site `web-org-tukuk` serves nothing here — the apex is a
# proxied CNAME to tunnel `c1d8ea0f…`) and no GitHub Pages target (the
# `_github-pages-challenge-tukuk` TXT proves ownership only; deleting it un-verifies the domain).
